# One More Time Around....

Just over a year ago- Spring semester of 2025, to be exact, I distinctly recall sitting in a class
and feeling like databses would never be my thing. I would never be a databses guy. It started with
ERD's-- for those of you not in the know, those are Entity-relationship diagrams. the terminology 
alone had me on the ropes. This is before we had even gotten to the execution. by the end of the 
semester, I had done well. an A+. but I didn't feel any closer to understanding what I was doing. 
I most certainly didn't feel capable. 

  A lot of people might think a classroom is the wrong setting, or that the professor was perhaps
lacking. I can assure you with absolute enthusiasm and certainty-- The professor and the class 
were not the problem. The problem was internal, and even further than that, the problem wasn't a 
problem at all. It was willing to come in its own time. If things like that came at beck and call,
I suppose the vast majority of us might be high-powered geniuses in action. 

  This all goes back to a particular experience - or grouping of experiences- with video games. Probably 
not the direction you were expecting, but it's the first immediately recognizable analog. I recall being 
godawful at video games more often than not, growing up (don't ever expect me to admit that out loud.) 
I would play games, put them down after failing or dying everal times, and not return to them. At least 
not at the time- but I found, more often than not, that returning to a game years later would produce 
dramatically different results. 

  Then it's college- at 33 years old, learning introductory databases concepts- or doing my best to 
learn them anyway. Now, at a much older and wiser 34 I look back on that time, and it finally clicks. 
the information I gained had amalgamated into a nice reference for when i decided to sit in front of my 
computer and play with SQL once more. All of the commands are plain english-- in the most literal sense. 
nothing about the syntax feels foreign. The things i do have to touch up in memory often land with an 
underwhelming sense of "well yeah, obviously." 

  My unifying theory then, on this particular front, is that a lot more things than we give credit for
probably deserve one more time around. The danger (and there is danger) in declaring yourself bad at 
something too early, in my experience anyway- is that the verdict tends to stick much longer than the 
evidence that would otherwise support it. We treat ourselves like the bad guy far too often, or convince
ourselves we aren't doing alright. 

  Be kind to yourself- but be kind to yourself with purpose. Be kind in the sense that you want you to 
succeed-- not in that you end up coddling yourself, or otherwise convincing yourself that the things 
you were no good at are somehow beneath you. we're all just people which gets a lot more messy than it 
sounds. It's okay to fail and keep trying. it's also okay to fail, set the infernal thing down, and 
return to it when you've put some distance between yourself and it. 

  Last spring, ERDs had me on the ropes. Last night, i was able to sit at a terminal and issue orders 
to MariaDB like a weird little tyrant with a fresh schema. The machine compiled. There wasn't any fanfare
or grand revalation. There wasn't a secret database gene that had activated in my sequence. It just needed
time to mature. 

  Dont be afraid to give former beasts of burden another hearing. some of them lost their teeth and claws 
in the interim. some of them were never monsters at all. And some of them, apparently, are just tables waiting
for a primary key to be declared. 
